    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes. The full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured audit event for owner creation, keeping each concern in its own small unit.\n\n**New types** (`.../rest/audit/`):\n- **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** \u2014 an immutable `record {seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}` with a `toJson()` renderer (Jackson 3 `JsonMapper`). Its static factory `of(seq, owner, asOf)` is the *single place* that reads the owner's primary identifier \u2014 currently `customerCode`. When that identifier is later unified into a member id, only this factory changes and the event automatically carries the new value. Membership level reuses the existing `MembershipLevels.levelOf`.\n- **`OwnerEventSequence`** \u2014 a `@Component` wrapping an `AtomicLong`, giving a monotonically increasing `next()` shared across all creates for the application's lifetime.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- `AuditOwnerCreated.service` now takes the `OwnerEventSequence` bean (injected by type, like other Spring beans in the pipeline) and, right after the existing human-readable audit line, emits `OwnerCreatedEvent.of(sequence.next(), owner, asOf).toJson()` on the same `AUDIT` logger. The function's single responsibility \u2014 auditing a created owner \u2014 is unchanged; it just emits both representations. No YAML change was needed since the step and its `@Val Owner` were already in place.\n\nVerified output:\n```\nINFO AUDIT - {\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\n`AUDIT.info(json)` with no placeholder args logs the JSON verbatim (SLF4J only substitutes `{}` when arguments are passed), so the braces are safe. `AcceptanceTest` and the full suite pass.",
